C20|C08,C09|HSF.IF.B.4|structured_response|Increasing, decreasing, positive and negative intervals, extrema and end behavior for loaded nonperiodic families; actual representation required.|Describe key features with interval and endpoint precision.|For f(x)=(x-1)^2-4, state where f is decreasing and where it is negative.|Decreasing on (-infinity,1); negative on (-1,3).|Distinguish an interval of inputs from a range of outputs or a sign of slope.
P10|P03,P04,P06|HSA.APR.A.1|reasoning|Closure under addition, subtraction and multiplication; division is not generally closed.|Justify closure using nonnegative integer exponents and give a division counterexample.|Are polynomials closed under division by nonzero polynomials? Explain.|No; dividing the polynomial 1 by the nonzero polynomial x gives 1/x, which is not a polynomial in x.|Contrast valid polynomial division with a guarantee that every quotient is a polynomial.
G13|G02,L05,X05|HSF.LE.A.1.a|reasoning|Equal input intervals; fixed additive or multiplicative change derived from a rule, not assumed from a finite table.|Justify the difference and ratio laws symbolically.|For f(x)=mx+b and g(x)=a*c^x with a nonzero and c positive, compare f(x+1)-f(x) and g(x+1)/g(x).|The difference is m; the ratio is c. Both are independent of x under the stated conditions.|Distinguish an observed pattern from its general algebraic justification.
